window.MH_TR["Ch00_Introduction"]={"t":"Introduction: the map","l":[[1.0,"a long way, from a block of raw material to a final shape with precise dimensions."],[9.0,"the milling machine, grinding, and sometimes wire EDM or a laser."],[18.0,"depth: how it works, what it can deliver, and what makes it expensive."],[25.0,"of processes exist, and how to choose between them."],[31.0,"There are three main ways to give a part its shape."],[34.0,"material changes shape, but almost nothing is removed."],[41.0,"You can add material, layer by layer. That's additive manufacturing."],[45.0,"Or you can remove material from a larger block until the shape you want is left. That's machining."],[51.0,"This series covers all three."],[53.0,"We'll start with how the raw material is made: casting, forging and extrusion."],[58.0,"From there we'll move on to machining, sheet metal work, and the non-conventional processes."],[63.0,"And at the end, we'll get to additive manufacturing."],[67.0,"Material removal itself has several families."],[71.0,"In machining, a tool with a sharp edge and a defined geometry shears a chip from the material."],[76.0,"In abrasive machining, thousands of small, hard grains each scrape off a tiny chip."],[83.0,"without a cutting edge: with an electric spark, a beam of light, a jet of water, or a plasma arc."],[93.0,"Every machining operation has two motions."],[95.0,"The primary motion creates the cutting speed."],[98.0,"The feed motion advances the tool into fresh material."],[102.0,"On a lathe, the part rotates and the tool moves along it."],[106.0,"On a milling machine, the tool rotates, and the part or the tool moves along the axes."],[112.0,"Three parameters will follow us throughout the series."],[115.0,"cutting edge passes over the material, in meters per minute."],[123.0,"Feed is how far the tool advances per revolution, or per tooth."],[127.0,"Depth of cut, ap, is how much material is removed in a single pass."],[133.0,"The chip itself tells you a lot: its color, shape and length show whether the parameters are right."],[140.0,"A grinding wheel is made of very hard grains, held together by a bond."],[145.0,"Each grain removes very little material. So grinding is relatively slow, but very accurate."],[151.0,"Grinding also suits hardened material, which an ordinary tool struggles to cut."],[157.0,"Honing and lapping are the next step, for even higher finish and accuracy."],[162.0,"In EDM, electric sparks melt away tiny particles of the material."],[167.0,"The tool never touches the part, so the hardness of the material hardly matters."],[172.0,"The one condition is that the material conducts electricity."],[175.0,"A laser melts the material with a focused beam of light."],[179.0,"A waterjet carrying abrasive grains cuts almost any material, with no heat-affected zone."],[185.0,"Plasma cuts conductive metals fast, but with lower accuracy."],[190.0,"Choosing a process starts with five questions."],[193.0,"What is the shape? A round part usually starts on a lathe, and a prismatic part on a milling machine."],[199.0,"What is the material, and how hard is it?"],[201.0,"What accuracy and finish are required?"],[204.0,"Turning and milling typically reach IT7 to IT9, with a surface roughness Ra of 0.8 to 3.2 microns."],[214.0,"Grinding reaches IT5 to IT6, and a roughness Ra of 0.2 to 0.8 microns."],[223.0,"Honing and lapping go below that."],[225.0,"What is the quantity? A single part and a batch of thousands won't be made the same way."],[231.0,"In large batches, it often pays to forge or cast close to the final shape, and save on machining."],[237.0,"And what is the total cost, not just the machine time?"],[241.0,"Take a steel pin that must be hardened, with a precise diameter."],[245.0,"We turn it while the material is still soft, and leave a small machining allowance."],[250.0,"Then it goes through heat treatment."],[252.0,"And finally, we grind it to its final size."],[255.0,"That's how most parts go through a chain of processes, not just one."],[259.0,"In every chapter, we'll also ask what makes the process expensive."],[263.0,"operations like heat treatment or moving between machines."],[270.0,"And one important rule: the tighter the tolerance, the longer and more expensive the process."],[276.0,"This is the series map: 25 chapters in six groups."],[280.0,"In the next chapter, we'll start at the beginning: casting."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch01_Casting"]={"t":"Casting","l":[[1.0,"Casting is one of the oldest ways to make metal parts, and it is still one of the most common."],[7.0,"An engine block, a pump housing, a valve body and a turbine blade all start life as castings."],[13.0,"cavity shaped like the part, and wait for it to solidify."],[21.0,"But to get a sound part, a lot of details have to be controlled."],[25.0,"Every casting has a mold, and inside it a cavity in the shape of the part."],[29.0,"The cavity is formed with a pattern, a replica of the part, made of wood, plastic or metal."],[36.0,"Internal cavities, like a hole or a channel, are formed with a core placed inside the mold."],[42.0,"The mold is usually made in two halves, and the plane between them is called the parting line."],[47.0,"The metal enters through a gating system: a sprue, runners, and gates into the cavity."],[53.0,"There is also a riser: a reservoir of liquid metal that keeps feeding the part while it solidifies."],[60.0,"Metal shrinks in three stages."],[63.0,"As the liquid cools, as it turns solid, and as the solid keeps cooling down to room temperature."],[70.0,"one: if there's nowhere to draw extra metal from, a cavity forms inside the part."],[79.0,"keeps flowing from it into the part until the very end."],[86.0,"dimensions, so the pattern is made larger than the part."],[92.0,"About 1% for gray cast iron, about 1.3% for aluminum, and about 2% for steel."],[99.0,"A part meant for casting is designed differently from a part machined from solid."],[104.0,"Walls need a draft angle, usually one to three degrees, so the pattern can be pulled out."],[109.0,"thick section solidifies last, and that's where cavities tend to form."],[117.0,"sharp corner concentrates stress and can crack during cooling."],[124.0,"machining allowance is left: a few millimeters of material above the final size."],[133.0,"The most common method is sand casting."],[135.0,"pattern, the pattern is pulled out, and the metal is poured."],[141.0,"After solidification the mold is broken, and every casting needs a new mold."],[146.0,"from a few kilograms to tens of tons, and almost any metal."],[155.0,"The drawback: a rough surface, around Ra 12.5 to 25 microns, and low accuracy."],[162.0,"So almost every functional surface on a sand casting is machined."],[167.0,"In investment casting, also called lost-wax casting, the pattern is made of wax."],[173.0,"It is coated with several layers of ceramic and heated: the wax melts out, leaving a ceramic shell."],[179.0,"Metal is poured into it, and the shell is broken off after solidification."],[185.0,"There's no parting line and no need for draft, so very complex shapes and fine details are possible."],[192.0,"The surface is much smoother, around Ra 1.6 to 3.2 microns, and the accuracy is high."],[199.0,"This is how turbine blades, medical implants, and parts in steels, superalloys and titanium are made."],[206.0,"The price: a long process, a lot of labor, and a high cost per part."],[212.0,"When quantities are large, it pays to invest ina steel die that serves thousands of castings."],[218.0,"In permanent mold casting, the metal flows into the die by gravity."],[223.0,"In die casting, the metal is injected into the die at high pressure and high speed."],[228.0,"This gives thin walls, precise dimensions, and a very short cycle per part."],[233.0,"The method mainly suits metals with a low melting point: aluminum, zinc and magnesium."],[239.0,"But the die is very expensive, and only pays off in large quantities."],[244.0,"And because of the fast injection, air gets trapped in the metal."],[248.0,"welded or heat treated: the trapped gas expands and forms blisters."],[257.0,"Castings have a few typical defects."],[259.0,"Gas porosity: small bubbles of gas trapped in the metal."],[264.0,"Shrinkage cavities: voids in the places that solidified last without feeding."],[269.0,"Misrun: the metal solidified before filling the whole cavity, usually in thin walls."],[276.0,"Hot tears: cracks that form when the metal shrinks and the mold restrains it."],[281.0,"the tool opens a void that was hidden below the surface."],[287.0,"Critical parts are inspected by X-ray."],[290.0,"So what does this mean for whoever machines castings?"],[293.0,"The outer layer is hard and abrasive: sand grains and an oxide skin wear down the cutting edge."],[300.0,"So the first pass has to be deep enough to get below the skin."],[303.0,"so the points on the casting used for that setup are defined in advance."],[311.0,"After the first pass, work continues from machined surfaces."],[315.0,"What makes a casting expensive?"],[317.0,"steel die. Its cost is spread over the number of parts."],[325.0,"Cores: every core is more work and another risk of a defect."],[329.0,"after machining has cost both the casting and the machining."],[336.0,"gating system, cleaning, and grinding off excess metal."],[343.0,"Inspections, like X-ray, and heat treatment when required."],[347.0,"And the machining allowance: every millimeter left on the casting is machine time."],[353.0,"So how do you choose?"],[354.0,"For a few parts, it is often cheaper to machine from solid, or to sand cast."],[359.0,"sand casting, or investment casting when accuracy is needed."],[367.0,"For thousands of aluminum parts or more: die casting."],[370.0,"flow that follows the shape of the part, and it may contain tiny voids."],[378.0,"So it's usually weaker and less fatigue resistant than a forged part of the same material."],[383.0,"From here we'll move on to the next chapter: forging."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch02_Forging"]={"t":"Forging","l":[[1.0,"a compressor blade: all parts that must carry high loads and fatigue for years."],[9.0,"Most of them start as forgings."],[11.0,"the solid state, by the force of a hammer or a press."],[18.0,"And the result is a stronger part than the same part made by casting or machined from solid."],[24.0,"Metal is made of grains, tiny crystals."],[27.0,"When it is shaped by force, the grains are stretched along the direction the material flows."],[33.0,"This creates grain flow, like the grain in wood."],[36.0,"In a forged part, the grain follows the shape of the part, and wraps around corners and shoulders."],[42.0,"In a part machined from solid, the cut crosses the grain exactly in those areas."],[47.0,"from casting the raw material, and the structure becomes finer and more uniform."],[56.0,"The result: higher strength, fatigue resistance and impact resistance."],[62.0,"Most forging is done hot."],[64.0,"the grains rebuild themselves while it is being shaped."],[74.0,"degrees, and for aluminum about 400 to 500 degrees."],[82.0,"The drawback: scale forms on the surface, a hard oxide layer, and the dimensions are less precise."],[89.0,"In cold forging, the shaping is done at room temperature."],[92.0,"precise dimensions and a clean surface, and the material work-hardens as it is shaped."],[100.0,"This is how screws and rivets are made, for example, in huge quantities."],[106.0,"There's also warm forging, which combines some of the advantages of both."],[111.0,"In open-die forging, the material is squeezed between simple dies, usually flat ones."],[117.0,"The material isn't enclosed on all sides, and the operator turns and moves it between blows."],[123.0,"This is how material is drawn out, upset and thickened, to make large shafts, rings and discs."],[130.0,"The tooling is cheap, and parts weighing tens of tons and more can be forged."],[135.0,"But the accuracy is low, and a large machining allowance is left."],[140.0,"In closed-die forging, each part has a pair of steel dies, with a cavity shaped like the part."],[146.0,"A hot billet is placed between the dies, and they close on it, forcing the material to fill the cavity."],[153.0,"To make sure the cavity fills completely, a little more material is used than needed."],[158.0,"The excess is squeezed out between the dies, forming a thin rim around the part called flash."],[164.0,"The flash cools quickly, gets hard, and restricts further outward flow."],[169.0,"That way it pushes the material to fill the corners."],[172.0,"At the end, the flash is trimmed off in a separate press."],[176.0,"goes through several stations, from rough shaping to the final shape."],[185.0,"The dies are expensive, so the method pays off in batches."],[189.0,"The force comes from a hammer or a press."],[192.0,"A hammer strikes fast, many short blows, and the material flows mainly near the surface."],[198.0,"A press squeezes slowly and continuously, and the shaping penetrates deeper into the part."],[206.0,"A part for forging is also designed differently."],[209.0,"angles to release the part, usually 5 to 7 degrees on the outer walls."],[217.0,"Generous radii at the corners, so the material flows without getting stuck and forming a lap."],[223.0,"And no ribs that are too tall and thin, which are hard to fill."],[226.0,"And a machining allowance on every precise surface, which also covers the scale layer."],[233.0,"Forging has its typical defects too."],[235.0,"A lap: the material folds over on itself and forms a hidden crack, usually near a sharp corner."],[242.0,"Underfill: there wasn't enough material or heat, and the corners didn't fill."],[247.0,"relative to each other, and the two halves of the part don't line up."],[254.0,"And cracks, when material is forged too cold."],[257.0,"Critical forgings are inspected with ultrasound, to find internal defects."],[263.0,"So what does this mean for whoever machines forgings?"],[266.0,"As with castings, the scale is hard and abrasive, so the first pass has to get below it."],[271.0,"Walls with draft aren't parallel, so they're hard to hold in an ordinary vise."],[276.0,"where the first setup is located, so it is checked before starting."],[282.0,"A forged part also has internal stresses: removing a lot from one side can distort it."],[288.0,"symmetrically, leaving a finishing pass for the end."],[295.0,"What makes forging expensive?"],[297.0,"The dies: tool steel, precision machining, and they wear and need repair and replacement."],[303.0,"Heating: energy, furnaces and time."],[306.0,"heated and trimmed, and never ended up in the part."],[312.0,"Trimming the flash, removing the scale, and heat treatment."],[316.0,"Inspections like ultrasound."],[318.0,"And the machining allowance, again: every millimeter is machine time."],[323.0,"So when should you forge?"],[324.0,"When the part carries high loads, fatigue or impact, and when its failure is dangerous."],[330.0,"material and machining time compared with machining from solid."],[338.0,"But complex internal shapes, cavities and thin walls are hard to forge, and there casting wins."],[346.0,"And for a single part, it's usually simpler to machine from solid, or from an open-die forging."],[351.0,"In the next chapter we'll meet another way to shape metal by force: extrusion."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch03_Extrusion"]={"t":"Extrusion","l":[[1.0,"Rails, tubes, window frames, and heat sinks with dozens of fins."],[6.0,"They all have one thing in common: a constant cross-section along their whole length."],[11.0,"Most of them are made by extrusion."],[13.0,"the material comes out through an opening, and takes the shape of the opening."],[22.0,"A cylindrical billet of metal is heated and loaded into a container."],[26.0,"die, which has an opening in the shape of the cross-section."],[33.0,"The metal flows through the opening, and comes out the other side as a long profile."],[38.0,"billet and that of the profile is called the extrusion ratio."],[45.0,"and a single billet produces a profile tens of meters long."],[53.0,"This way you get shapes that are very hard to make any other way, in almost unlimited length."],[59.0,"There are two main ways to extrude."],[61.0,"In direct extrusion, the ram pushes the billet, and it slides along the container wall."],[67.0,"force is highest at the start, when the billet is longest."],[74.0,"relative to the container, and the die is pushed into it."],[80.0,"There's no friction with the wall, so less force is needed, but the equipment is more complex."],[87.0,"Most extrusion is done hot."],[89.0,"degrees, and it is the most common material for extrusion."],[96.0,"higher temperatures and forces, and special lubrication."],[107.0,"In cold extrusion, at room temperature, a small slug is squeezed in a fast blow."],[113.0,"This is how aerosol cans, tubes and battery casings are made, for example."],[119.0,"How do you extrude a tube, or a profile with closed cavities?"],[122.0,"A special die is used, with a mandrel held in place by thin bridges."],[127.0,"The metal splits around the bridges, and rejoins in the chamber around the mandrel before it exits."],[133.0,"Where the streams rejoin, weld lines form along the profile."],[137.0,"but in critical parts these lines are taken into account."],[144.0,"extruded around a central mandrel, giving a tube with no weld lines."],[152.0,"The profile comes out hot, and is cooled in air or water."],[156.0,"Then it is stretched slightly, to straighten it and relieve stresses."],[160.0,"It is cut to length, and many aluminum alloys are age hardened, which raises their strength."],[166.0,"extruded to the end: it is cut off and sent for recycling."],[173.0,"A profile for extrusion is also designed by rules."],[177.0,"section flows faster than a thin one, and the profile bows."],[184.0,"Rounded corners, so the material flows easily and the die doesn't crack."],[189.0,"And no deep, narrow channels: the part of the die that forms them is thin and long, and can break."],[197.0,"A symmetric, smaller profile is easier to extrude, and gets a tighter tolerance."],[203.0,"Extrusion has its typical defects too."],[206.0,"Die lines: fine streaks along the profile, from wear or dirt on the die."],[212.0,"Surface cracks, when extruding too fast or too hot."],[216.0,"Blisters, from air or gas trapped between the billet and the container."],[221.0,"And twist and bow, when the flow isn't balanced."],[225.0,"So what does this mean for whoever machines profiles?"],[228.0,"A profile isn't perfectly straight and flat: it has bow and twist, within the tolerance of the standard."],[234.0,"rather than relying on raw surfaces for precise dimensions."],[241.0,"clamping forces, so they are supported and not clamped too hard."],[248.0,"final, usually only short operations are left: cutting to length, drilling and local milling."],[258.0,"What makes extrusion expensive?"],[260.0,"even a custom profile pays off in medium quantities."],[269.0,"But there is a minimum quantity: whole billets are extruded, giving tens of meters at a time."],[275.0,"Scrap: the billet butt, and the ends cut off after stretching."],[279.0,"A complex profile, with cavities and thin walls, is extruded more slowly, and its die wears faster."],[287.0,"And extra operations: age hardening, anodizing or painting, and machining."],[293.0,"So when should you extrude?"],[295.0,"When the part is long, and has a constant cross- section."],[299.0,"When you can buy a profile close to the final shape, and save a lot of machining from solid."],[304.0,"And when the material suits it, mainly aluminum."],[307.0,"That completes the ways of making the raw material and the initial shape."],[311.0,"In the next chapter we'll start machining, with the most common process: turning."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch04_Turning"]={"t":"Turning","l":[[1.0,"Shafts, pins, bushings and screws: almost every round part goes through a lathe."],[7.0,"Turning is the most common machining process."],[10.0,"along the part or toward its center, removing a layer of material."],[17.0,"operations, the tools, and what determines accuracy and cost."],[24.0,"On the left is the headstock, with the main spindle that rotates the part."],[28.0,"A chuck with three jaws that grip the part is mounted on the spindle."],[33.0,"moves in two axes: along the part, and perpendicular to it."],[39.0,"On machines, the length axis is usually called Z, and the diameter axis X."],[46.0,"On the other side is the tailstock, with a center that supports the end of a long part."],[51.0,"In chapter 0 we met three parameters: cutting speed, feed and depth of cut."],[57.0,"In turning, the cutting speed depends on the spindle speed and on the diameter of the part."],[62.0,"travels a longer distance each revolution, so its cutting speed is higher."],[69.0,"cutting speed of 200 meters per minute, you need about 1,270 rpm."],[79.0,"When facing toward the center, the diameter gets smaller, and the cutting speed drops."],[84.0,"speed: the machine raises the spindle speed as the diameter gets smaller, up to a preset limit."],[93.0,"revolution, and depth of cut is how much is removed from the radius in a single pass."],[102.0,"Removing one millimeter from the radius makes the diameter two millimeters smaller."],[108.0,"A lathe can do many operations."],[110.0,"Longitudinal turning reduces the diameter along the part."],[114.0,"Facing squares off the end of the part."],[116.0,"Grooving creates a groove, and parting cuts the part off the bar."],[121.0,"Drilling and boring create precise bores in the center of the part."],[125.0,"And threading creates a thread, when the feed per revolution equals the pitch exactly."],[131.0,"You can also turn tapers, radii and profiles, with both axes moving together."],[137.0,"Most tools today are a holder with an indexable insert, made of carbide or another hard material."],[143.0,"angle is stronger, and one with a sharp angle reaches tight spots."],[150.0,"For example, an 80-degree rhombic insert suits roughing, and a 35-degree insert suits profiling."],[158.0,"The nose has a radius: a large radius and a low feed give a smoother surface."],[163.0,"But a radius that is too large increases the radial forces, and can cause chatter."],[169.0,"And the insert has a chip breaker: a shape that bends the chip and breaks it into short pieces."],[175.0,"Turning accuracy starts with workholding."],[178.0,"A three-jaw chuck centers the part by itself, but not perfectly."],[181.0,"bored to the exact diameter of the part, on the machine itself."],[188.0,"For small parts and bar stock, a collet is used, which grips all around and is very accurate."],[194.0,"A long, slender part deflects under the cutting force."],[198.0,"the chuck exceeds 3 times the diameter, the end is supported with a center."],[206.0,"And a very long part gets a steady rest, which supports it in the middle."],[210.0,"The chip tells you what is happening in the cut."],[213.0,"tool, scratch the surface, and are dangerous to the operator."],[220.0,"Short chips, shaped like a spiral or a C, are a sign of correct parameters."],[225.0,"depth of cut, or choose an insert with a different chip breaker."],[232.0,"On a CNC lathe, a program controls the axes, and the turret changes tools by itself."],[239.0,"A turning center also has live tools: drills and milling cutters that rotate in the turret itself."],[245.0,"This way the part can be drilled and milled without taking it off the machine."],[249.0,"spindle to spindle, and its other side is machined too, without another setup."],[257.0,"guide bushing, so the cut is always close to the support."],[264.0,"This is how small, long, precise parts are made."],[267.0,"Ordinary turning typically gives an accuracy of IT7 to IT9, and a roughness Ra of 0.8 to 3.2 microns."],[277.0,"parameters, better accuracy and roughness can be reached."],[284.0,"A big advantage of turning: everything turned in the same setup rotates around the same axis."],[290.0,"So diameters that must share an axis, like bearing seats, are turned in one setup."],[296.0,"Turning has its typical problems too."],[298.0,"on a slender part or with a holder that sticks out too far."],[305.0,"Built-up edge: soft material sticks to the insert, and the surface becomes rough."],[310.0,"The common fix: raise the cutting speed."],[313.0,"barrel-shaped, because where it bends away the insert cuts less."],[320.0,"batch, so it is measured and corrected in the program."],[328.0,"What makes turning expensive?"],[329.0,"Setup time: jaws, tools, the program, and a first part for inspection."],[335.0,"Cycle time: how much material has to be removed, and how fast the material allows you to cut."],[341.0,"Stainless steel, titanium and superalloys are turned much more slowly than ordinary steel."],[347.0,"measurements, and sometimes grinding afterward."],[354.0,"same setup, and on an ordinary lathe they need another machine."],[363.0,"Turning suits every round part, from a small pin to a shaft meters long."],[368.0,"And when the part has flats, pockets and non- round shapes, it moves on to the next machine."],[374.0,"In the next chapter: milling."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch05_Milling"]={"t":"Milling","l":[[1.0,"Flat surfaces, pockets, slots and free-form shapes: most parts that aren't round are milled."],[7.0,"Milling is the opposite of turning: the tool rotates, and the part or the tool moves along the axes."],[14.0,"A milling cutter has several teeth, and each tooth cuts a small chip every revolution."],[20.0,"tools, the parameters, and what makes a milled part expensive."],[27.0,"On a vertical milling machine, the spindle stands upright, and the tool cuts from above."],[33.0,"The table moves in two axes, X and Y, and the spindle moves up and down, in the Z axis."],[40.0,"swaps a tool in seconds, and everything is controlled by a program."],[48.0,"There are also horizontal mills, where the spindle lies flat; they suit large parts and clear chips well."],[55.0,"part can be reached, and we'll expand on that in chapter 21."],[62.0,"The most common tool is the end mill: it cuts both on its side and at its tip."],[67.0,"For large surfaces, a face mill with indexable inserts is used."],[72.0,"For curved surfaces and molds, a ball end mill with a round tip is used."],[77.0,"Milling tools are usually made of solid carbide or high-speed steel, or carry indexable inserts."],[83.0,"materials, and a tool with few teeth leaves room for chips, and suits aluminum and roughing."],[92.0,"and spindle speed, but the diameter is the tool's, not the part's."],[99.0,"For example, a 10 mm tool at a cutting speed of 150 meters per minute needs about 4,800 rpm."],[108.0,"tool advances while one tooth passes through the material."],[116.0,"The table feed rate is the feed per tooth, times the number of teeth, times the spindle speed."],[121.0,"In our example, with 4 teeth and 0.05 mm per tooth, that's about 960 mm per minute."],[129.0,"along the tool, and width of cut, how much of the tool diameter is engaged in the material."],[138.0,"There are two ways the tool can meet the material."],[141.0,"In conventional milling, the tooth enters thin and exits thick."],[146.0,"At first it slides and rubs, which heats it and wears it."],[150.0,"In climb milling, the tooth enters thick and exits thin."],[154.0,"Wear is lower and the surface is better, so on CNC machines this is usually the choice."],[160.0,"On old machines with backlash in the screw, climb milling can pull the table."],[166.0,"Milling can do many operations."],[168.0,"Face milling flattens a large surface."],[171.0,"Shoulder milling creates a wall and a floor at a right angle."],[174.0,"In a slot or a pocket, the tool plunges into the material and clears a closed shape."],[180.0,"Contour milling follows the outline of the part."],[183.0,"And a machining center also drills, taps, and machines curved surfaces."],[189.0,"Most parts are held in a vise, or in a fixture dedicated to the part."],[193.0,"measured from, and every additional setup can add error."],[198.0,"important surfaces as possible are machined in the same setup."],[205.0,"And every setup you save also saves setup time."],[209.0,"Milling has its typical problems too."],[211.0,"A long, slender tool deflects: the wall comes out out of square, and the tool may break."],[217.0,"than 3 to 4 times its diameter, reduce the depth of cut and the feed."],[225.0,"Chatter leaves marks on the surface, mainly on thin walls."],[229.0,"In deep pockets, chips that aren't cleared get recut, and can break the tool."],[233.0,"And every edge of a cut leaves a burr, which has to be removed."],[238.0,"The tool is round, so an internal corner in a pocket will always have a radius."],[243.0,"If the drawing calls for a sharp internal corner, another process is needed, like EDM."],[249.0,"tool radius, so the tool passes through the corner without stopping."],[255.0,"A deep, narrow pocket needs a long, thin tool, which is slow and risky for the tool."],[262.0,"And every additional side to machine is another setup, and more time."],[266.0,"What makes milling expensive?"],[268.0,"The number of setups: every setup means setup time, a vise or a fixture, and inspection."],[274.0,"Programming: a complex part takes programming time and verification of the toolpath."],[280.0,"Cycle time: how much material is removed, and what the part is made of."],[285.0,"Small tools in deep pockets, and thin walls, force you to work slowly."],[290.0,"And tight tolerances, deburring, and measurement."],[294.0,"Turning and milling are the two core processes of machining."],[297.0,"Turning for round parts, and milling for everything else: flats, pockets, slots and shapes."],[303.0,"And a single part often needs both."],[305.0,"In the next chapter: drilling."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch06_Drilling"]={"t":"Drilling","l":[[1.0,"Almost every part has holes: for screws, for pins, for fluid passages, or for assembling other parts."],[8.0,"Drilling is the most common operation in machining, and usually the first one on every hole."],[14.0,"But a drill alone gives limited accuracy, so a whole family of tools surrounds it."],[20.0,"The common drill is the twist drill, with two cutting lips at the tip."],[24.0,"The helical flutes carry the chips out of the hole, and let coolant in."],[28.0,"high-speed steel drill, and usually 130 to 140 ona carbide drill."],[38.0,"At the center of the tip is a section that doesn't cut well, so a long drill tends to wander as it starts."],[44.0,"To start the hole exactly on target, it is spot drilled first, with a short, rigid drill."],[51.0,"drill diameter and the spindle speed, and the feed is in millimeters per revolution."],[59.0,"almost zero, and there the material is pushed more than it is cut."],[66.0,"So the thrust force in drilling is high."],[68.0,"The deeper the hole, the harder it is to clear the chips."],[72.0,"diameter, peck drilling is used: go in, retract to clear chips, and go back."],[81.0,"much greater depths, and very deep holes use gun drills."],[89.0,"A drill makes a hole fast, but not very accurately."],[92.0,"nominal diameter, and its surface is relatively rough."],[99.0,"The position and straightness of the hole also depend on a good start and a rigid drill."],[103.0,"a precise hole, like a pin or bearing seat, another tool follows."],[111.0,"And this is the drilling family."],[113.0,"a precise diameter, usually to an H7 tolerance, and a smooth surface."],[122.0,"But a reamer follows the existing hole, and doesn't correct its position."],[127.0,"bored with a boring head, which also corrects position and straightness."],[134.0,"A counterbore creates a flat seat for a screw head, so the head doesn't stick out."],[139.0,"a conical head, and is also used to deburr the edge of the hole."],[146.0,"We'll cover threading a hole in the next chapter."],[149.0,"A through hole goes from side to side, and a blind hole stops inside the material."],[155.0,"At the bottom of a blind hole a cone is left, at the angle of the drill tip."],[160.0,"If a flat bottom is needed, a special tool is required, and that adds cost."],[165.0,"And in a blind hole, the chips have to come out through the same opening the drill went in."],[170.0,"Drilling has its typical problems too."],[173.0,"Wandering at the start of the hole, mainly when the drill enters an inclined or curved surface."],[179.0,"Chips packing in the flutes break drills, especially small ones."],[183.0,"A burr at the exit of a through hole, which has to be removed."],[186.0,"And a worn drill cuts poorly, heats up, and gives an oversize or out-of-round hole."],[193.0,"Holes are worth designing properly too."],[195.0,"Use standard diameters, which have an off-the- shelf drill."],[199.0,"Give the drill a surface perpendicular to it, at the entry and at the exit."],[204.0,"Avoid, where possible, very deep holes with a small diameter."],[208.0,"need it, because every precision hole adds an operation."],[215.0,"What makes drilling expensive?"],[217.0,"every diameter is another tool and another tool change."],[224.0,"Deep holes, which need pecking or special tools."],[228.0,"Precision holes, which need reaming or boring after drilling."],[232.0,"Hard materials, and small drills that break."],[235.0,"And deburring, especially where holes meet inside the part."],[240.0,"Drilling creates the hole, and its family gives it accuracy and shape."],[245.0,"diameter, boring for a precise position, and the counterbore for the screw head."],[252.0,"In the next chapter: threading."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch07_Threading"]={"t":"Threading","l":[[1.0,"Almost every mechanical product is held together with screws."],[5.0,"Threading creates a thread: a helical groove, internal in a hole or external on a shaft."],[12.0,"the ways to make it, and what's important to know when designing a threaded part."],[19.0,"A thread is a groove that winds around an axis, like a helix."],[24.0,"threads, and on an ordinary thread it is also the distance the screw advances in one turn."],[32.0,"The most common thread is the metric thread, with a 60-degree angle between the flanks."],[38.0,"For example, M10 is a 10 mm thread, with a coarse pitch of 1.5 mm."],[44.0,"There are also fine threads, with a smaller pitch, for example M10 with a pitch of 1.25 mm."],[51.0,"that seal, and trapezoidal threads for motion, like a machine's lead screw."],[59.0,"tolerance classes. The common ones: 6H for the nut, and 6g for the bolt."],[68.0,"An internal thread in a hole is usually made with a tap."],[72.0,"First a hole of the right diameter is drilled, and the tap cuts the threads into the walls of the hole."],[78.0,"about the thread diameter minus the pitch: for M10, drill 8.5 mm."],[88.0,"A tap with spiral flutes pulls the chips up, and suits blind holes."],[92.0,"A spiral point tap pushes the chips forward, and suits through holes."],[96.0,"displaces the material into the shape of the thread."],[102.0,"it needs a slightly larger hole, and a ductile material."],[109.0,"It's hard to remove, sometimes only by EDM, and sometimes the part is scrapped."],[118.0,"Another way is thread milling."],[120.0,"a circular, helical path, advancing one pitch per revolution."],[128.0,"One tool suits many diameters with the same pitch, and the chips are small."],[133.0,"And if the tool breaks, it's smaller than the hole and easy to remove."],[138.0,"So it's a good choice for expensive parts, hard materials and large diameters."],[143.0,"An external thread can be turned or rolled."],[146.0,"makes several passes, going a little deeper each time."],[153.0,"The machine synchronizes the feed with the rotation, so every pass lands exactly in the groove."],[160.0,"with the thread form press on the bar, and the material flows into the shape of the threads."],[169.0,"The thread is stronger, because the grain isn't cut, and the process is very fast."],[174.0,"This is how most standard screws are made."],[177.0,"Threads are worth designing properly too."],[179.0,"diameter is enough, and in aluminum about twice the diameter."],[189.0,"Beyond that, the extra threads carry almost no load, and only add time and risk."],[195.0,"In a blind hole, drill deeper than the thread, to leave room for the tap's tip and for chips."],[200.0,"On an external thread that runs up to a shoulder, design a thread relief groove, so the tool can exit."],[207.0,"thread, so the screw enters easily and the first thread doesn't get damaged."],[214.0,"closed many times, a thread insert is installed to reinforce the thread."],[223.0,"Threads are inspected with go and no-go gauges."],[226.0,"side must enter all the way, and the no-go side shouldn't enter more than a few turns."],[235.0,"For an external thread a ring gauge is used, on the same principle."],[241.0,"Threading has its typical problems too."],[243.0,"A broken tap, mainly in small blind holes, and in hard or sticky materials."],[248.0,"inspection, because of a worn tap or a wrong hole diameter."],[255.0,"Torn threads, when the chips don't clear or there isn't enough lubrication."],[260.0,"And a burr at the start of the thread, which stops the screw from entering."],[264.0,"What makes threading expensive?"],[266.0,"The number of threads and different sizes: every size is another drill and another tap."],[272.0,"Small threads in deep blind holes, which need careful work."],[276.0,"Hard materials, like titanium and stainless steel, and the risk of a broken tap in an expensive part."],[283.0,"Tight tolerance classes, special threads, and thread inserts."],[287.0,"And gauge inspection of every thread."],[290.0,"For an internal thread: a tap, and in expensive or hard parts, thread milling."],[296.0,"For an external thread: turning, and in large quantities, rolling."],[301.0,"In the next chapter: grinding."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch08_Grinding"]={"t":"Grinding","l":[[1.0,"When the tolerance is very tight, the surface must be smooth, or the material is hardened, you grind."],[8.0,"In grinding, thousands of small, hard grains each scrape off a tiny chip."],[14.0,"So grinding is relatively slow, but very accurate."],[18.0,"A grinding wheel is made of three things: grains, a bond, and pores."],[23.0,"for most steels, silicon carbide for cast iron and non-ferrous metals."],[32.0,"For very hard steels, CBN is used, and for carbide and ceramics, diamond."],[37.0,"The bond holds the grains, and the pores leave room for chips and coolant."],[42.0,"Coarse grains remove a lot of material, and fine grains give a smoother surface."],[48.0,"And there's a rule that seems backward: for a hard material, choose a soft wheel."],[53.0,"out, and a new sharp grain is exposed. That way the wheel sharpens itself."],[61.0,"on a magnetic table, and moves back and forth under the wheel."],[69.0,"In cylindrical grinding the part rotates between centers, and the wheel grinds the diameter."],[75.0,"In internal grinding, a small wheel goes into a bore and grinds it from the inside."],[80.0,"between two wheels and on a work rest blade, with no centers and no clamping."],[88.0,"This is how pins are ground fast, in large quantities."],[92.0,"The wheel spins very fast, usually at a surface speed of 30 to 35 meters per second."],[99.0,"But each pass removes very little, usually a few hundredths of a millimeter."],[104.0,"At the end comes spark-out: a few passes with no feed, until the sparks almost disappear."],[110.0,"Coolant is vital: almost all the energy in grinding turns into heat."],[116.0,"Grinding usually reaches an accuracy of IT5 to IT6, and a roughness Ra of 0.2 to 0.8 microns."],[126.0,"It also gives good geometric form: roundness, flatness and straightness."],[132.0,"And it suits hardened materials, after heat treatment, when an ordinary cutting tool struggles."],[138.0,"Over time the wheel goes dull and gets loaded, and its shape changes."],[142.0,"So it is dressed: a diamond tool removes a thin layer and exposes sharp grains."],[148.0,"The wheel is also balanced, because imbalance causes vibration and marks on the part."],[153.0,"marked on the wheel. A wheel that bursts is very dangerous."],[161.0,"Grinding has its typical problems too."],[163.0,"hardness of the surface, and sometimes creates tiny cracks. It isn't always visible to the eye."],[172.0,"Chatter marks, from an unbalanced wheel or a poorly supported part."],[177.0,"A loaded wheel, mainly in soft materials, which rubs instead of cutting."],[181.0,"And a tapered or out-of-round part, when the machine or the support isn't aligned."],[187.0,"Grind only the surfaces that really need it."],[190.0,"tenths of a millimeter, because each pass removes very little."],[197.0,"At a shoulder corner, the wheel has a radius, so design a relief groove or a suitable radius."],[203.0,"And harden the part before grinding, because heat treatment after grinding can distort it."],[209.0,"What makes grinding expensive?"],[211.0,"Slow material removal: every unnecessary tenth of a millimeter means more passes."],[217.0,"Setup time, dressing and balancing."],[220.0,"Dedicated machines, and a skilled operator."],[223.0,"Precise inspection, and sometimes a check for grinding burn."],[227.0,"the end, after the part has already been through all the other processes."],[234.0,"Grinding is the stage where a part gets its final accuracy and finish."],[239.0,"It's relatively slow and expensive, so it is used only where it's needed."],[244.0,"In the next chapter: broaching."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch09_Broaching"]={"t":"Broaching","l":[[1.0,"A keyway in the bore of a gear, splines inside a bushing, or a hexagonal hole."],[7.0,"These shapes are very hard to make by turning or milling."],[10.0,"Broaching makes them in a single pass."],[13.0,"The tool is called a broach: a long bar with many teeth in a row."],[17.0,"Each tooth is slightly higher than the one before it, usually by a few hundredths of a millimeter."],[24.0,"each tooth removes another thin layer, and the last tooth leaves the final shape."],[32.0,"the semi-finishing teeth, and at the end finishing teeth of equal size, which size the shape."],[41.0,"At the front of the tool is a pilot, which enters the pre-drilled hole and aligns the tool."],[46.0,"Between the teeth is a chip gullet, and the chip stays in it until the tooth leaves the part."],[52.0,"So the length of cut is limited: in a part that's too long, the gullet fills up."],[58.0,"pilot hole, and turns it into the required shape: a keyway, splines, a square or a hexagon."],[68.0,"In external broaching, the tool passes over the surface of the part, creating flats and profiles."],[74.0,"This is how, for example, the slots in turbine discs that hold the blade roots are made."],[80.0,"In job shops, a keyway is often cut with a manual broach kit and a simple press."],[85.0,"And there's also rotary broaching, done on a lathe or a mill, which suits hexagons in blind holes."],[92.0,"It's a single pass, so the cycle time is very short, often seconds."],[97.0,"The accuracy is good, usually IT7 to IT8, and the surface is good."],[103.0,"And every part comes out like the one before, because the shape is in the tool."],[107.0,"The tool is expensive, and suits only one shape and one size."],[112.0,"So broaching pays off mainly in volume, except for standard keyway broaches."],[117.0,"In ordinary internal broaching, the hole must be a through hole."],[121.0,"And the cutting forces are high, so the part must be strong, and its walls not too thin."],[127.0,"Broaching has design rules too."],[129.0,"A through hole is preferred, with a reasonable length of cut relative to the tool."],[134.0,"The pilot hole must have the right diameter and tolerance, because the tool starts from it."],[139.0,"And if the slot must stop inside the part, a relief groove is needed, or another process."],[146.0,"Broaching has its typical problems too."],[148.0,"A broken tool, mainly when the gullets fill up or the material is too hard."],[152.0,"A keyway that's shifted or not parallel to the axis, when the part isn't aligned well."],[158.0,"Chatter marks on the surface."],[160.0,"And a burr where the tool exits."],[163.0,"What makes broaching expensive?"],[165.0,"The tool: long, with many teeth, and made specially for the shape."],[170.0,"Resharpening the tool, and maintaining it."],[172.0,"A dedicated machine for high forces."],[175.0,"And in small quantities, wire EDM or a slotting machine is sometimes cheaper."],[182.0,"Broaching produces precise internal and external shapes in a single pass."],[187.0,"The tool is expensive, but in large volumes the cost per part is very low."],[193.0,"In the next chapter: gears."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch10_Gears"]={"t":"Gears","l":[[1.0,"Gears transmit motion and torque between shafts, in gearboxes, engines and machines."],[7.0,"To run quietly and last, the tooth form has to be very precise."],[13.0,"That's why gears are made on special machines with special tools."],[17.0,"when two teeth roll on each other, the speed ratio stays constant."],[25.0,"The size of the tooth is set by the module."],[27.0,"number of teeth: a gear with module 2 and 30 teeth has a pitch diameter of 60 mm."],[36.0,"The standard pressure angle is 20 degrees."],[39.0,"quietly, bevel gears for shafts at an angle, and worm gears for a large speed reduction."],[48.0,"The common method is hobbing."],[50.0,"cutting teeth, and it rotates in sync with the gear, so the teeth are formed by a generating process."],[58.0,"Hobbing is fast and precise, and suits most external gears, with spur or helical teeth."],[65.0,"In gear shaping, a gear-shaped cutter moves up and down and rotates together with the part."],[71.0,"It can also cut internal gears, and teeth near a shoulder, where a hob has no room."],[76.0,"separately with a form cutter, but the accuracy is lower."],[84.0,"And internal splines are often made by broaching, as we Saw in the previous chapter."],[89.0,"Most gears are hardened, to resist wear and load."],[93.0,"surface takes up carbon and is hardened, while the core stays tough."],[99.0,"precision gears the teeth are ground after hardening."],[106.0,"Gears are inspected for profile, pitch and runout, on a dedicated measuring machine."],[112.0,"In the shop, a span measurement across several teeth, or a measurement over pins, is often used."],[118.0,"The accuracy grade is set by a standard, and the tighter it is, the more expensive the gear."],[125.0,"Gears have their typical problems too."],[127.0,"Noise and vibration, when the tooth profile or the pitch isn't accurate."],[132.0,"Pitting: small pits on the tooth surface, from contact fatigue."],[136.0,"Wear, when the hardening or the lubrication isn't enough."],[139.0,"And heat-treat distortion, which changes the geometry."],[144.0,"It's best to choose a standard module, which has ready-made tools."],[147.0,"below about 17 teeth the tool undercuts the tooth root and weakens it."],[156.0,"Leave room for the tool to run out near a shoulder, or choose gear shaping."],[160.0,"And don't demand a higher accuracy grade than you really need."],[165.0,"What makes gears expensive?"],[166.0,"Dedicated machines, and tools that suit a specific module."],[170.0,"Heat treatment, and tooth grinding after it."],[173.0,"Inspection on a dedicated measuring machine."],[176.0,"sometimes better to buy a standard gear and machine it."],[183.0,"Gears need special tools and machines, heat treatment and precise inspection."],[188.0,"That completes the machining group."],[190.0,"In the next chapter we'll start sheet metal work: sheet metal fabrication."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch11_Sheet_Metal_Fabrication"]={"t":"Sheet metal fabrication","l":[[1.0,"brackets, trays and chassis: they all start as a flat sheet."],[8.0,"Fabrication turns flat sheet into parts: cutting, punching, bending, joining and finishing."],[15.0,"next chapters we'll expand on some of the processes."],[22.0,"Sheet metal is thin relative to its size, usually up to about 6 mm thick."],[28.0,"Above that, we usually talk about plate."],[30.0,"In sheet metal work almost no material is removed: the flat shape is cut out, and bent."],[36.0,"So it's fast and cheap, and the part's thickness is the same everywhere."],[41.0,"The common materials: mild and galvanized steel, stainless steel and aluminum."],[46.0,"when bent to a small radius, so you choose an alloy that bends well, or a larger radius."],[56.0,"This is what the typical workflow looks like."],[58.0,"The part is designed, and the flat pattern is calculated: the flat shape, before bending."],[63.0,"The flat pattern is cut and punched out of a sheet."],[66.0,"It's bent, joined, and finally finished, for example painted or coated."],[71.0,"When sheet metal is bent, the inside of the bend is compressed, and the outside is stretched."],[77.0,"In the middle there's a layer whose length doesn't change: the neutral axis."],[82.0,"During bending, this axis shifts toward the inside."],[86.0,"Its position is described by the K factor, usually between 0.3 and 0.5."],[92.0,"So the flat pattern is shorter than the sum of the part's outside dimensions."],[97.0,"factor depends on the material, thickness and tooling, so a first part is checked."],[105.0,"The flat pattern can be cut in several ways."],[108.0,"A shear cuts straight lines, fast and cheap."],[110.0,"A turret punch holds dozens of tools, and punches holes and shapes quickly."],[116.0,"It can also form small features, like ventilation louvers."],[120.0,"good accuracy, so it suits prototypes and many kinds of parts."],[128.0,"Laser, waterjet and plasma get their own chapters, and punching comes next."],[133.0,"Most bends are made on a press brake."],[135.0,"A punch presses the sheet into a V-shaped die, and the depth it reaches sets the angle."],[141.0,"After bending, the sheet springs back slightly."],[145.0,"So it's bent a little past the required angle."],[147.0,"Every material and thickness has a minimum bend radius, and below it the outside cracks."],[153.0,"rolling direction of the sheet, because the risk of cracking is lower."],[160.0,"We'll expand on bending in chapter 13."],[164.0,"When cylinders and cones are needed, the sheet is rolled between rolls, and that's chapter 14."],[171.0,"and stamped in a die, in a single blow or over several stations."],[178.0,"We'll expand on that in chapter 15."],[181.0,"Sheet metal parts are joined in several ways."],[184.0,"Spot welding joins two sheets at points, with electric current, fast and with no added material."],[191.0,"TIG, gives a strong, sealed joint, but heats and distorts the sheet, and often needs grinding."],[201.0,"Rivets, including blind rivets installed from one side, join without heat."],[206.0,"are pressed into a hole in the sheet, giving a strong thread even in thin sheet."],[215.0,"Sheet metal fabrication has design rules too."],[219.0,"A hole too close to a bend line gets distorted."],[221.0,"A rule of thumb: keep the hole at least 2.5 times the thickness, plus the bend radius, from the bend."],[229.0,"small relief cut is made, so the sheet doesn't tear at the corner."],[235.0,"A flange that's too short isn't held well by the die, and doesn't bend correctly."],[241.0,"And it's best to use the same radius on every bend, to work with a single tool."],[245.0,"And don't expect machining tolerances: every bend adds deviation."],[251.0,"At the end, the edges are deburred, and the part is finished to suit the material and use."],[257.0,"galvanized. Aluminum is anodized, and stainless steel is passivated or polished."],[266.0,"Note: paint and coatings add thickness, which matters in holes and in assembly."],[272.0,"What makes sheet metal fabrication expensive?"],[275.0,"The bends: every bend is an operation, and every tool change on the press brake is setup time."],[282.0,"Welding and grinding: a lot of manual work, and sometimes straightening after distortion."],[288.0,"Tooling: stamping needs an expensive die, while a laser needs no tooling at all."],[293.0,"Material utilization: how many parts fit on one sheet, and how much goes to scrap."],[299.0,"And finishing, installing press-in hardware, and tight tolerances."],[304.0,"So how do you choose?"],[305.0,"For prototypes and small batches: laser and a press brake, with no special tooling."],[311.0,"For medium batches: a turret punch, and bending."],[315.0,"For large quantities: die stamping, which produces a part with every stroke."],[320.0,"And when the part is thick, massive or very precise, machining is the better choice."],[326.0,"In the next chapter: punching."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch12_Punching"]={"t":"Punching","l":[[1.0,"An electrical cabinet panel has dozens of holes, cutouts and louvers."],[6.0,"goes through the sheet and back out in a fraction of a second."],[12.0,"In this chapter we'll see how it works, what it delivers, and what makes it expensive."],[17.0,"Punching has two parts: a punch on top, and a die below."],[22.0,"clearance, usually 5 to 10 percent of the sheet thickness per side."],[29.0,"The punch comes down, presses the sheet, and shears it."],[32.0,"burnished band forms, and below it a rough fracture zone."],[38.0,"And on the exit side, below the sheet, a burr forms."],[42.0,"Too little clearance wears the tool, and too much clearance makes the burr bigger."],[48.0,"When the piece that falls out is scrap, and the hole is what remains, that's piercing."],[53.0,"When the piece that falls out is the part itself, that's blanking."],[57.0,"The principle is the same; the difference is what you keep."],[61.0,"A turret punch press holds dozens of tools of different shapes and sizes."],[66.0,"machine selects a tool and punches thousands of hits per hour."],[72.0,"with small, closely spaced hits along the line: that's nibbling."],[80.0,"stiffening ribs, extruded collars for tapping, and countersinks."],[88.0,"On some machines the tools rotate, so the same tool can punch at several angles."],[94.0,"hole, the sheet thickness and the strength of the material."],[99.0,"For example, a 20 mm hole in 2 mm steel needs about 4.5 tons."],[106.0,"So in thick sheet or in stainless steel, the force rises fast."],[111.0,"Punching is very fast on holes and shapes that repeat."],[114.0,"The accuracy is good, usually around a tenth of a millimeter."],[118.0,"And the same machine can also form, which a laser can't do."],[122.0,"Punching has design rules too."],[124.0,"than the sheet thickness, and in stainless steel a larger diameter is needed."],[132.0,"A hole too close to an edge or to another hole can distort or tear the sheet."],[136.0,"It's best to use standard sizes and shapes, which have ready-made tools."],[141.0,"And remember that the burr is on one side, and mark on the drawing which side it's allowed on."],[146.0,"Punching has its typical problems too."],[149.0,"A large burr, from a worn tool or the wrong clearance."],[152.0,"Sheet distortion, when there are many closely spaced holes."],[156.0,"Slug pulling: the cut-out piece comes back up with the punch and falls onto the sheet."],[161.0,"And a broken tool, mainly with small holes in thick sheet."],[165.0,"What makes punching expensive?"],[167.0,"Tooling: a standard tool is cheap, but a special shape needs a special tool."],[172.0,"Setup time and tool changes."],[175.0,"Nibbling large shapes, which takes many hits."],[178.0,"And deburring, and forms that need dedicated tools."],[182.0,"So when punching, and when laser?"],[184.0,"Punching suits many identical holes, batches, and parts that need forming."],[190.0,"Laser suits complex shapes and prototypes, with no tooling."],[194.0,"And there are combination machines that punch and laser cut in the same setup."],[198.0,"In the next chapter: bending."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch13_Bending"]={"t":"Bending","l":[[1.0,"Almost every sheet metal part has bends."],[4.0,"a flat sheet bends by hand, but after one bend it's already much stiffer."],[12.0,"how to reach an accurate angle, and what makes bending expensive."],[18.0,"A press brake has a punch on top, and a V-shaped die below."],[22.0,"pushes the sheet into the V, without pressing it all the way to the bottom."],[30.0,"The angle is set by how deep the punch goes, so the same die can produce many angles."],[35.0,"the bottom of the die. The angle is more accurate, but much more force is needed."],[44.0,"moves automatically and sets where the bend line will be."],[50.0,"A rule of thumb: the V opening is about 8 times the sheet thickness."],[55.0,"In air bending, the inside radius is set mainly by the V opening, not by the punch."],[61.0,"The thicker the sheet, the faster the force rises, and the wider the V, the lower the force."],[67.0,"So for thick sheet a wide V is chosen, and the result is a larger radius."],[73.0,"After the punch goes up, the sheet springs back slightly."],[76.0,"In aluminum and stainless steel the springback is larger than in mild steel."],[81.0,"correct this, and some measure the angle during the bend."],[88.0,"The flange must be long enough to rest on both shoulders of the V."],[92.0,"The wider the V, the longer the minimum flange."],[95.0,"early bend can collide with the punch or the machine on the next bend."],[102.0,"For boxes and deep channels, a gooseneck punch is used, which leaves room for the flange."],[109.0,"panel benders, which bend all the sides automatically."],[116.0,"As we saw in chapter 11, the flat pattern is shorter than the sum of the outside dimensions."],[122.0,"The CAD software calculates it using the K factor, and a first part is checked and corrected."],[128.0,"Bending has its typical problems too."],[130.0,"small, or when the bend line runs parallel to the rolling direction."],[138.0,"machine deflects in the middle. This is corrected by crowning."],[145.0,"Die marks on the surface. On visible parts, protective film or a special die is used."],[151.0,"And a hole close to the bend line, which distorts."],[155.0,"A bend angle usually reaches an accuracy of half a degree to one degree."],[160.0,"A dimension between two bends is less accurate than a laser-cut dimension."],[165.0,"bends stacks up, so it's best to measure from the same side, and not demand machining accuracy."],[173.0,"What makes bending expensive?"],[175.0,"The number of bends: every bend is another operation."],[178.0,"Tool changes, and a complex bend sequence."],[182.0,"Tall flanges and deep boxes, which need special tools."],[186.0,"Tight tolerances, and inspections."],[188.0,"And large panels, which need two operators."],[192.0,"Bending turns a flat sheet into a stiff part, at the required angle and shape."],[197.0,"flanges and a feasible sequence, avoids most of the problems."],[205.0,"In the next chapter: plate rolling."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch14_Plate_Rolling"]={"t":"Plate rolling","l":[[1.0,"Tanks, large pipes, rings and cones usually start as a flat plate."],[6.0,"In plate rolling, the plate passes between rolls, and curves gradually until it closes into a cylinder."],[13.0,"A three-roll machine has one top roll and two bottom rolls."],[17.0,"the top roll is lowered slightly, and the diameter gets smaller."],[25.0,"On a four-roll machine, an extra roll grips the plate, and the work is faster and more accurate."],[31.0,"On a three-roll machine, the ends of the plate stay flat, because they don't pass between all the rolls."],[37.0,"So the ends are pre-bent, on a press brake or ona four-roll machine."],[41.0,"Without that, after welding there's a flat strip along the seam, and the cylinder isn't round."],[47.0,"a rectangle, and the rolls are tilted relative to each other."],[54.0,"It takes more experience, and sometimes a special machine."],[59.0,"Here too the plate springs back, so it is rolled gradually and checked."],[63.0,"The diameter is checked with a template, or by measuring the circumference."],[68.0,"Finally, the seam is welded along the cylinder."],[71.0,"rolled again after welding, to restore the roundness."],[78.0,"The minimum diameter depends on the top roll diameter and the plate thickness."],[83.0,"Thick, wide plates need a very large, powerful machine."],[87.0,"And hard materials spring back more, and make an accurate diameter harder to reach."],[93.0,"Plate rolling has its typical problems too."],[96.0,"Flat ends, when they aren't pre-bent."],[98.0,"A barrel or hourglass shape, when the rolls deflect in the middle."],[103.0,"Ovality, when the diameter isn't uniform around the circumference."],[107.0,"And ends that don't meet exactly at the seam."],[111.0,"What makes plate rolling expensive?"],[113.0,"The thickness and width of the plate, which determine the machine needed."],[117.0,"Pre-bending the ends, and the number of passes."],[120.0,"Welding the seam, and re-rolling after it."],[123.0,"And roundness and diameter checks."],[125.0,"Plate rolling turns a flat plate into a cylinder or a cone, in several gradual passes."],[132.0,"Pre-bending the ends and re-rolling after welding are the key to good roundness."],[137.0,"In the next chapter: stamping."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch15_Stamping"]={"t":"Stamping","l":[[1.0,"Car doors, beverage cans, sinks, brackets and electrical contacts."],[6.0,"on the sheet, and in a second a finished part comes out."],[13.0,"Stamping is the cheapest way to make a sheet metal part, but only in large quantities."],[18.0,"dies, deep drawing and embossing, and understand when it pays off."],[25.0,"A mechanical press drives the tool with a flywheel. It's very fast, and suits mass production."],[32.0,"over the whole stroke, so it's good for deep drawing."],[39.0,"the air, and slow exactly when the tool meets the sheet."],[47.0,"Press size is measured in tons, by the maximum force it applies."],[51.0,"The die is the heart of the process."],[54.0,"It has a punch on top and a die below, with guide pins that align them precisely."],[58.0,"A stripper plate holds the sheet and strips it off the punch after every stroke."],[63.0,"expensive, and takes weeks, sometimes months, to build and try out."],[71.0,"A simple die performs one operation per stroke."],[75.0,"In a progressive die, a strip of sheet moves through several stations inside the same die."],[81.0,"piercing, bending, forming, and finally cutting off the part."],[88.0,"station, and pilot holes make sure the strip advances precisely."],[94.0,"So every stroke of the press produces a finished part."],[98.0,"each part is cut off at the start, and arms move it between the stations."],[107.0,"Deep drawing turns a flat blank into a cup, a box, or the body of a container."],[113.0,"material from the flange flows inward to form the walls."],[120.0,"To keep the flange from wrinkling, a blank holder presses on it during the draw."],[124.0,"Too little pressure causes wrinkles, and too much tears the sheet near the punch radius."],[130.0,"diameter can be about twice the cup diameter, no more."],[137.0,"A taller cup needs several draws, one after another."],[140.0,"And beverage cans add ironing: passing through narrow rings that thin the wall and make it longer."],[147.0,"Embossing presses a shape into the sheet without cutting it."],[150.0,"It creates raised and recessed features: stiffening ribs, markings, a logo."],[156.0,"A single rib along a flat panel greatly increases its stiffness, while adding almost no weight."],[163.0,"inside a closed die, giving sharp details and a precise thickness, like on coins."],[172.0,"Not every sheet suits stamping: for deep forming, a soft, ductile sheet made for forming is chosen."],[180.0,"Stainless steel and aluminum are harder to form, and tend to stick to the die."],[185.0,"So oil or a lubricant is used, which reduces friction and prevents scratches."],[191.0,"And here too the sheet springs back, and this is corrected in the die design."],[196.0,"Stamping has design rules too."],[198.0,"Generous radii at every corner, and at every transition between wall and bottom."],[204.0,"corners are better than a deep box with sharp corners."],[210.0,"Holes far enough from formed areas, or they'll distort."],[214.0,"And it's best to design the part so all operations are done from the same direction."],[219.0,"Stamping is highly repeatable: after a thousand parts, the part still matches the first."],[225.0,"Stamping has its typical problems too."],[228.0,"Wrinkles in the flange, when the blank holder pressure is too low."],[232.0,"Tearing in the wall or the bottom, when the draw is too deep or the radius too small."],[237.0,"Earing on the rim of a drawn cup, because the sheet isn't the same in every direction."],[243.0,"Scratches and galling, when lubrication is missing."],[246.0,"And burrs and dimensional changes, as the die wears."],[251.0,"What makes stamping expensive?"],[253.0,"First of all, the die: design, manufacturing and tryout, before the first part comes out."],[259.0,"A press big enough for the required force."],[261.0,"Die changes and setup time."],[263.0,"And die maintenance, because it wears over time."],[266.0,"low, so in quantities of thousands and up, stamping almost always wins."],[274.0,"For prototypes and small quantities, laser cutting and a press brake are better."],[280.0,"Stamping turns sheet into a finished part in a second, using a die designed for a single part."],[287.0,"The die is expensive, but in large quantities no method is cheaper."],[291.0,"That completes the sheet metal group."],[293.0,"In the next chapter we'll start the non-conventional processes, with wire EDM."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch16_EDM_Wire"]={"t":"EDM wire","l":[[1.0,"Stamping punches and dies, extrusion dies, hardened gears, and nearly sharp inside corners."],[8.0,"All of these can be cut with a thin wire, without the tool touching the part and without cutting forces."],[14.0,"This is wire EDM: electrical discharge machining."],[19.0,"spark removes metal, what the process delivers, and what makes it expensive."],[27.0,"The wire is one electrode, and the part is the other."],[31.0,"Between them there's a tiny gap, filled with deionized water, which doesn't conduct electricity."],[37.0,"The machine sends a voltage pulse, and a spark jumps between the wire and the part."],[41.0,"The spark melts and vaporizes a microscopic amount of metal, and the water flushes it away."],[48.0,"second, and that's how the wire advances and cuts."],[54.0,"The wire is also eroded by the sparks, so it's fed continuously from a spool, and used only once."],[61.0,"The wire is usually brass, with a common diameter of a quarter of a millimeter."],[67.0,"The only condition: the material must conduct electricity."],[70.0,"Steels, hardened tool steel, carbide, titanium, Inconel, copper and graphite are all suitable."],[78.0,"Hardness doesn't matter at all: hardened material cuts almost like soft material."],[83.0,"But ceramics and plastics, which don't conduct, can't be cut this way."],[88.0,"The table moves in two axes, and the wire runs between an upper guide and a lower guide."],[94.0,"lower one, the wire tilts, so you can cut a taper, or a different shape at the top and bottom."],[102.0,"To cut a closed internal shape, a small start hole is drilled first, and the wire is threaded through it."],[108.0,"so they can move from shape to shape without an operator."],[114.0,"Usually there's one rough cut, followed by several skim cuts."],[118.0,"remove a thin layer and improve accuracy and surface finish."],[126.0,"With several skim cuts, an accuracy of a few microns is reached."],[131.0,"The kerf the wire leaves is slightly wider than the wire itself, because of the spark gap."],[136.0,"So an inside corner will always have a small radius, about half the kerf width."],[141.0,"Speed is measured in area: length x height, in mm? per minute. Tall parts cut slower."],[149.0,"surface that melted and resolidified: the recast layer."],[155.0,"This layer is hard and brittle, and can contain micro-cracks."],[159.0,"important in parts loaded in fatigue, like in aerospace."],[167.0,"The classic application: stamping punches and dies, as we saw in chapter 15."],[172.0,"Also extrusion dies, gears and splines in hardened parts."],[176.0,"Keyways in a part that has already been hardened."],[179.0,"Small, precise parts, and very thin walls, because there are no forces to bend them."],[184.0,"And nearly sharp inside corners, which milling can't produce."],[189.0,"Wire EDM has design rules too."],[192.0,"only through shapes can be cut. A blind pocket needs sinker EDM, in the next chapter."],[200.0,"An internal shape needs a start hole, and room for it."],[203.0,"a small tab is sometimes left, and removed at the end."],[210.0,"it's needed, because every extra skim cut is more time."],[217.0,"Wire EDM has its typical problems too."],[220.0,"Wire breakage, from poor flushing or too much power."],[224.0,"stresses are released, mainly in hardened material."],[231.0,"Deviation along the height, when the wire vibrates or isn't tensioned enough."],[236.0,"And a recast layer that wasn't removed, which weakens the part."],[240.0,"What makes wire EDM expensive?"],[242.0,"Mainly time: the process is slow, and one part can take hours."],[247.0,"The area to cut, meaning length times height, and the number of skim cuts."],[252.0,"Wire, water, and machine maintenance."],[255.0,"And start holes and preparation."],[257.0,"On the other hand, the machine can run unattended all night, which lowers the cost a lot."],[262.0,"And for hardened or complex parts, there's no real substitute."],[267.0,"hardness, to an accuracy of microns, with no forces."],[275.0,"It's slow, so it's used where other processes can't do the job."],[279.0,"In the next chapter: sinker EDM."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch17_EDM_Sinker"]={"t":"EDM sinker","l":[[1.0,"An injection mold cavity, deep narrow ribs, and a blind pocket with sharp corners in hardened steel."],[8.0,"Milling can't reach there, and wire EDM can't cut a blind pocket."],[12.0,"In sinker EDM, a shaped electrode sinks into the part, and sparks remove the material."],[18.0,"In this chapter we'll get to know the electrode, the process, and what makes it expensive."],[23.0,"The principle is the same as wire EDM: sparks across a tiny gap melt and vaporize the material."],[31.0,"But here the electrode is a full shape, the mirror image of the cavity you want to produce."],[37.0,"conduct electricity, and the oil flushes the particles away."],[43.0,"A servo system holds the gap, and the electrode slowly descends into the material."],[49.0,"electrode moves in a small circle, enlarging the cavity under control and improving flushing."],[59.0,"The electrode is usually made of graphite or copper."],[63.0,"Graphite is light, relatively cheap, and machines fast, so it's common in large molds."],[69.0,"Copper gives a better surface finish and finer detail."],[72.0,"spark gap, and that gap differs between roughing and finishing."],[79.0,"usually there's one electrode for roughing, and one or two more for finishing."],[87.0,"Blind pockets of any shape, at any reasonable depth, in hardened material."],[93.0,"Sharp inside corners, as sharp as the electrode allows."],[97.0,"Deep, narrow ribs that no milling cutter can reach."],[100.0,"And uniform matte surfaces, which serve as the final finish of the product in molds."],[106.0,"shaped like a thin tube, with water flowing through it."],[113.0,"This drills small, deep holes fast in hard materials."],[117.0,"It's used for start holes for wire EDM, and for cooling holes in turbine blades."],[123.0,"Here too, a thin recast layer is left on the surface, with micro-cracks."],[128.0,"In sensitive parts it's removed by fine finishing or polishing."],[133.0,"Plastic injection molds are the most common application."],[137.0,"Die-casting and forging dies, as we saw in chapters 1 and 2."],[142.0,"Pockets and slots in hardened parts, after heat treatment."],[146.0,"And in aerospace, parts made of especially tough alloys."],[150.0,"Sinker EDM has design rules too."],[153.0,"electrode, so it's best to give a radius wherever possible."],[160.0,"Think about how the electrode will enter, and how the oil will flush the cavity."],[165.0,"drawing, because a finer finish takes more time and more electrodes."],[172.0,"Sinker EDM has its typical problems too."],[175.0,"Electrode wear, which rounds corners and changes dimensions."],[179.0,"process unstable and leaves burn marks on the surface."],[186.0,"And wrong dimensions, when the spark gap wasn't calculated correctly."],[191.0,"What makes sinker EDM expensive?"],[193.0,"The electrodes: design, milling and inspection, and sometimes that's the most expensive part."],[200.0,"Time: the process is slow, and one cavity can take hours, even days."],[206.0,"The number of electrodes, depending on the number of corners and the required finish."],[211.0,"On the other hand, the machine runs unattended, and in a hardened part there's no substitute."],[217.0,"Sinker EDM copies the shape of the electrode into the material, at any hardness."],[223.0,"blind pockets and sharp corners in hardened material, there's no substitute."],[231.0,"In the next chapter: laser."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch18_Laser"]={"t":"Laser","l":[[1.0,"Sheet metal parts of any shape, straight from the drawing, with no tooling and no dies."],[7.0,"This is laser cutting, one of the most common processes in shops today."],[11.0,"light cuts metal, what the laser delivers, and what makes it expensive."],[19.0,"A laser beam is focused to a tiny spot, where the energy density is enormous."],[25.0,"The metal melts, and sometimes vaporizes, along the cut line."],[30.0,"An assist gas blows the molten metal out through the kerf."],[34.0,"metal and adds heat, so cutting is fast, but the edge oxidizes."],[43.0,"nitrogen is used: the edge is clean and bright, but gas consumption is high."],[51.0,"And compressed air is a cheap compromise, for thin sheet."],[56.0,"efficient, fast, and able to cut even copper and brass, which reflect light."],[65.0,"still common for cutting wood, acrylic and non- metals."],[72.0,"Steel, stainless steel and aluminum, from thin sheet of half a millimeter up to thick plate."],[79.0,"Powerful machines cut steel 25 millimeters thick and more."],[84.0,"The kerf is very narrow, usually tenths of a millimeter."],[89.0,"The accuracy is good, usually around a tenth of a millimeter."],[93.0,"And the heat-affected zone is narrow, because the heat is concentrated."],[98.0,"On a flatbed machine, the cutting head moves over the sheet, and the motion is CNC controlled."],[104.0,"Nesting software arranges the parts on the sheet to leave as little scrap as possible."],[110.0,"A pallet changer lets a new sheet be loaded while the machine is cutting."],[114.0,"Tube lasers also cut holes and slots in the walls of tubes and profiles."],[120.0,"Fine striations can be seen on the edge, and in thick sheet they're more pronounced."],[125.0,"At the bottom, resolidified molten metal can remain: dross."],[130.0,"Where the beam pierces the sheet, a small mark is left."],[134.0,"And in thick sheet the edge is slightly tapered."],[138.0,"Marking and engraving: serial numbers, logos and codes, with no contact and no ink."],[144.0,"Laser welding: a narrow, deep seam, with little distortion."],[149.0,"And advanced uses, like cladding and metal 3D printing."],[153.0,"Laser cutting has design rules too."],[156.0,"with lower quality, and in thick sheet it's better to drill it."],[162.0,"Very small details overheat and can burn."],[165.0,"Tiny connections, microjoints, are left to hold the parts in the sheet until cutting is done."],[171.0,"Parts with a common line can be cut with a single cut, saving time and material."],[176.0,"oxide layer on the edge, which can interfere with paint and welding."],[183.0,"Laser cutting has its typical problems too."],[186.0,"Dross at the bottom of the cut, when the gas or the focus isn't right."],[190.0,"Burning at sharp corners in thick sheet."],[192.0,"Heat distortion of thin or small parts."],[195.0,"And reflective materials, which need a suitable laser."],[199.0,"What makes laser cutting expensive?"],[202.0,"Machine time: the cut length, the sheet thickness, and the number of pierce points."],[207.0,"Gas, mainly nitrogen in large quantities."],[210.0,"Sheet utilization: how well the parts are nested on it."],[214.0,"And removing dross and burrs, if needed."],[217.0,"But with no tools or dies, laser suits prototypes and small to medium batches."],[223.0,"A laser cuts almost any shape from sheet, fast, accurately, and with no tooling."],[229.0,"Thickness, gas and cut length set the price."],[233.0,"In the next chapter: waterjet cutting."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch19_Waterjet_Cutting"]={"t":"Waterjet cutting","l":[[1.0,"Rubber, stone, glass, composites, titanium and thick aluminum plate."],[7.0,"All of them can be cut on the same machine, with water."],[10.0,"cuts, what it delivers that no other process does, and what makes it expensive."],[18.0,"A pump pressurizes water to an enormous pressure, around 4,000 bar."],[23.0,"The water exits through a tiny orifice in a jewel, and becomes a thin, very fast jet."],[30.0,"A pure water jet cuts soft materials: rubber, foam, gaskets, and even food."],[36.0,"To cut metal, stone or glass, an abrasive sand is added to the jet, usually garnet."],[43.0,"The sand mixes with the water in a narrow tube, and it's the grains that do the cutting."],[48.0,"It's a cold process: no heat-affected zone, no hardening, and no heat distortion."],[54.0,"So it suits heat-sensitive materials, like titanium, hardened steel and composites."],[60.0,"It cuts almost any material, even very thick, sometimes more than 100 millimeters."],[66.0,"And there are no tools: like laser, you go straight from the drawing to the cut."],[71.0,"In thin sheet, waterjet cutting is much slower than laser."],[76.0,"The kerf is wider, usually around a millimeter."],[79.0,"out tapered, and the jet lags behind and rounds corners at the bottom."],[87.0,"Advanced heads tilt the jet and correct the taper."],[90.0,"Good accuracy is around a tenth of a millimeter."],[94.0,"And it's a wet, noisy process, with sand to clear away."],[99.0,"The part sits over a water tank, which absorbs the jet after it passes through."],[103.0,"The cutting head moves under CNC control, and on some machines it also tilts, to cut bevels."],[110.0,"And the cutting speed is chosen by the material, the thickness, and the required edge quality."],[116.0,"Gaskets, rubber and foam."],[118.0,"Stone and glass countertops."],[120.0,"Thick plates of aluminum and titanium."],[122.0,"Composites in aerospace."],[124.0,"And hardened steel parts that mustn't be heated."],[127.0,"Waterjet cutting has design rules too."],[130.0,"An inside corner will always have a radius, about half the kerf width."],[134.0,"In thick parts, account for the taper, or ask for taper correction."],[138.0,"risky, because the material can crack at the pierce point."],[144.0,"And small parts can drop or tip over, so tiny tabs are left."],[149.0,"Waterjet cutting has its typical problems too."],[152.0,"Taper and rounded corners at the bottom, when the cutting speed is too high."],[157.0,"A frosted edge on the side where the jet enters."],[160.0,"Delamination in composites, at the pierce point."],[163.0,"And flash rust on steel that sat in the water."],[166.0,"What makes waterjet cutting expensive?"],[169.0,"Machine time, which depends heavily on thickness and edge quality."],[174.0,"The abrasive, which is usually the largest running cost."],[177.0,"Maintenance of the pump, seals and orifices, which work at enormous pressure."],[182.0,"On the other hand, there are no tools, so it suits even a single part."],[187.0,"Waterjet cutting suits almost any material, at any reasonable thickness, with no heat."],[194.0,"isn't allowed, or the material is special, it's the choice."],[201.0,"In the next chapter: plasma."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch20_Plasma"]={"t":"Plasma","l":[[1.0,"Thick steel plate for bridges, ships and heavy machinery."],[5.0,"To cut it fast and cheaply, plasma is used."],[9.0,"what it delivers, and where it beats laser and waterjet."],[16.0,"Inside the torch there's an electrode, and an electric arc forms between it and the part."],[21.0,"Gas is forced through a narrow nozzle, and the arc heats it to more than twenty thousand degrees."],[27.0,"At that temperature the gas becomes plasma: an ionized gas that conducts electricity."],[33.0,"The plasma melts the metal, and the strong gas flow blows it out."],[38.0,"Because the arc passes through the part, the material must conduct electricity."],[43.0,"The gas can be air, oxygen, nitrogen, or special mixtures, depending on the material."],[50.0,"cheap and simple, and there are handheld units too."],[55.0,"High-definition plasma constricts the arc, giving a better edge and higher accuracy."],[61.0,"And there's also underwater cutting, which reduces noise, smoke and distortion."],[67.0,"Steel, stainless steel and aluminum."],[70.0,"It works best on plates from a few millimeters up to several tens of millimeters."],[75.0,"In thick plate it's much faster than laser and waterjet, and the machine is cheaper."],[80.0,"In thin sheet, a laser gives a better result."],[84.0,"The edge comes out slightly beveled, usually a few degrees."],[87.0,"The heat-affected zone is wider than with a laser."],[91.0,"Dross can remain at the bottom."],[92.0,"Accuracy is usually around half a millimeter, and better with high-definition plasma."],[98.0,"And a hole smaller than the plate thickness doesn't come out well, so it's better to drill it."],[103.0,"Steel structures and bridges."],[105.0,"Shipbuilding and heavy equipment."],[107.0,"Preparing weld bevels, with a tilting head."],[111.0,"And cutting pipes and profiles."],[113.0,"Plasma has design rules too."],[115.0,"Surfaces that must be precise are given a machining allowance."],[119.0,"The cut edge hardens from the heat, which can make later machining or bending harder."],[124.0,"Small, precise holes are drilled, not plasma cut."],[128.0,"Plasma has its typical problems too."],[130.0,"A beveled edge, when the speed or torch height isn't right."],[134.0,"Dross at the bottom, when the speed is too low or too high."],[138.0,"Falling quality, as the electrode and nozzle wear."],[142.0,"And heat distortion of thin plates."],[144.0,"What makes plasma cutting expensive?"],[147.0,"Consumables: electrodes and nozzles, which need frequent replacement."],[152.0,"Gas and electricity."],[153.0,"Cleaning off dross and grinding the edge."],[156.0,"And machining allowance, when accuracy is needed."],[159.0,"On the other hand, for thick steel plate, it's the cheapest way to cut."],[164.0,"Plasma cuts thick metal plate fast and cheaply, with moderate accuracy."],[169.0,"That completes the non-conventional group."],[171.0,"In the next chapter we'll start the advanced group: 5-axis milling."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch21_5-Axis_Milling"]={"t":"5-axis milling","l":[[1.0,"Impellers, turbine blades, aircraft structural parts, molds and medical implants."],[6.0,"Parts like these have complex surfaces that must be reached from every side, at every angle."],[13.0,"On an ordinary milling machine that takes many setups, many fixtures, and long tools."],[18.0,"With 5-axis milling, most of the work is done in one setup, with short tools and high accuracy."],[24.0,"two working methods, what happens behind the scenes, and what it costs."],[31.0,"An ordinary milling machine has three linear axes: X, Y and Z."],[36.0,"5-axis milling adds two rotary axes."],[40.0,"The A axis rotates around X, the B axis around Y, and the C axis around Z."],[46.0,"Each machine has two of them, depending on its configuration."],[50.0,"the table: the table tilts, and on it sits a rotating table, a trunnion."],[58.0,"medium parts: it's rigid and accurate, and the part turns to face the tool."],[66.0,"The drawback: the table has to tilt the full weight of the part, so it's limited in size."],[71.0,"head: the head tilts and rotates, and the part stays put."],[78.0,"This is the configuration for large, heavy parts, like aircraft structural parts and large molds."],[85.0,"and one in the head, a combination of the two approaches."],[92.0,"The most common method is called 3+2."],[95.0,"locks the rotary axes, and mills in three ordinary axes."],[102.0,"Then it tilts to the next angle, and continues."],[105.0,"setup, and only the side the part is held by remains."],[112.0,"3+2 suits angled holes, surfaces at angles, and box parts with many sides very well."],[119.0,"is rigid, because the rotary axes are locked during the cut."],[126.0,"In the simultaneous method, all five axes move together, continuously, while cutting."],[132.0,"This way the tool follows a curved surface, and keeps a constant angle to it all the way."],[138.0,"This is essential for impellers, blades, and any free-form surface."],[144.0,"cutting moves to a better zone of the ball, and the surface improves."],[155.0,"the full height of the wall, and get a smooth wall in one pass."],[162.0,"of a large circle, barrel cutters, which allow wide passes with a good finish."],[170.0,"When a rotary axis moves, the tool tip moves with it in space."],[174.0,"constantly calculates the linear motions that compensate for the rotation."],[182.0,"point management, and it lets the path be programmed by the tool tip, not by the machine."],[191.0,"where its pivot centers are, so it's calibrated regularly."],[198.0,"and 5-axis programming is more complex and takes experience."],[206.0,"translates the path into commands that specific machine understands."],[213.0,"There's a risk of collision between the tool, the holder, the head, the table, the part and the fixture."],[220.0,"So a full simulation of the machine, with its real model, is run before every cut."],[225.0,"rotary axes, and zones where the machine has to rotate very fast to pass a certain point."],[234.0,"In 5-axis milling, the workholding must leave access from every direction."],[239.0,"So the part is raised on a narrow fixture, so the head and the tool don't collide with the table."],[245.0,"move between machines without losing its position."],[251.0,"And often the part is machined from a solid block, leaving a small base that's cut off at the end."],[257.0,"Because everything is done in one setup, the relation between surfaces is more accurate."],[262.0,"But the errors of the rotary axes add up, so calibration matters."],[267.0,"Heat changes the machine during work, and advanced machines compensate for it."],[272.0,"machining, and checks dimensions along the way, without removing it."],[279.0,"Fewer setups: every additional setup can add error, aS we Saw in chapter 5."],[285.0,"doesn't need to be long to reach the depth, so it's more rigid and vibrates less."],[293.0,"Access to undercuts and angled surfaces that can't be reached in three axes."],[298.0,"Better surfaces, and less manual finishing."],[301.0,"And fewer fixtures, fewer transfers between machines, and a shorter lead time."],[307.0,"Impellers and blisks: a disc and blades from a single block."],[311.0,"Turbine blades, with curved surfaces and thin walls."],[315.0,"Aircraft structural parts, with deep pockets and angled walls."],[320.0,"Molds with deep cavities, where a short, tilted tool replaces a long, vibrating one."],[326.0,"Medical implants, and bodies with holes at many angles."],[330.0,"5-axis milling has design rules too."],[333.0,"Not every part needs 5 axes: if three axes are enough, it's cheaper."],[338.0,"each surface from, and don't create a surface no tool can reach."],[346.0,"Plan datums and tolerances so they can be machined in the same setup."],[350.0,"needed, because it's the most expensive to program and inspect."],[357.0,"5-axis milling has its typical problems too."],[360.0,"Collision, when the simulation doesn't match the real machine."],[364.0,"Marks on the surface, when the rotary axes move in jerks."],[368.0,"Dimensional errors, when the machine isn't calibrated."],[372.0,"And chatter in thin walls, even with a short tool."],[376.0,"What makes 5-axis milling expensive?"],[378.0,"The hourly rate is higher, because the machine is far more expensive."],[382.0,"Programming and simulation time, which on a first part can be longer than the machining itself."],[388.0,"A first part that needs to be inspected and tuned."],[390.0,"Special tools and holders."],[392.0,"On the other hand, you save setups, fixtures, transport between machines, and waiting time."],[399.0,"So it pays off mainly on complex, expensive parts, in small and medium quantities."],[404.0,"5-axis milling reaches almost any surface, in one setup and with short tools."],[411.0,"3+2 for parts with many sides and angles, and simultaneous for curved surfaces."],[416.0,"calibration and workholding, not just on the machine."],[423.0,"In the next chapter: mill-turn and Swiss-type."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch22_Mill-Turn_and_Swiss-Type"]={"t":"Mill-turn and Swiss-type","l":[[1.0,"A shaft with flats, a keyway and cross holes. A tiny medical screw. A long, slender hydraulic pin."],[9.0,"These are round parts, but they also need milling and drilling."],[12.0,"milling machine, they're made on a single machine."],[18.0,"machines: the mill-turn center, and the Swiss-type lathe."],[24.0,"rotating tools, driven by their own motor inside the turret."],[33.0,"The C axis lets the part stop at a precise angle, or rotate slowly during milling."],[38.0,"This makes it possible to mill flats and hexes, and to drill cross holes and off-center holes."],[45.0,"and pockets that don't pass through the center of the part."],[51.0,"A sub spindle grips the part after parting off, and machines the back side."],[56.0,"This way a completely finished part comes out, without anyone touching it."],[60.0,"Large machines also have a tilting milling head, the B axis, combining a lathe with 5-axis milling."],[68.0,"One setup, so the holes and flats are accurate relative to the axis of rotation."],[73.0,"Fewer transfers between machines, fewer fixtures, and fewer parts waiting between stations."],[79.0,"With a bar feeder, the machine can run unattended for many hours."],[84.0,"Live tools are weaker and less rigid than the spindle of a machining center."],[88.0,"So heavy milling and hard materials are better done on a milling machine."],[93.0,"collision between the turret, the spindles and the part."],[99.0,"And the machine is more expensive than an ordinary lathe."],[103.0,"Swiss-type is a different kind of lathe, born in the Swiss watch industry."],[107.0,"On an ordinary lathe, the part rotates in place and the tool moves along it."],[113.0,"and forth through a guide bushing, which supports it."],[119.0,"the part is supported just a few millimeters from the cutting point."],[125.0,"twenty times longer than their diameter, without bending."],[132.0,"Swiss machines usually work with bar stock up to 32 millimeters in diameter."],[137.0,"They have many fixed tools, live tools, a sub spindle, and many axes working at the same time."],[143.0,"And they reach accuracy of microns, in mass production."],[148.0,"The bar must be ground to a precise diameter, so it slides smoothly in the bushing."],[153.0,"The material left in the bushing at the end of the bar goes to scrap."],[157.0,"For short parts there's a mode without the bushing, which saves scrap."],[161.0,"diameter: a very large flange doesn't suit Swiss- type."],[169.0,"Medical and dental screws and implants."],[172.0,"Connectors and pins for electronics."],[175.0,"Parts for watches and precision instruments."],[178.0,"Hydraulic pins, shafts and valves."],[180.0,"And on mill-turn centers: shafts, bushings, valve bodies, and flanges with holes."],[186.0,"A simple round part, with no milling: an ordinary lathe."],[191.0,"A round part with flats, slots and holes: a mill-turn center."],[195.0,"A small, long, slender part, in large quantities: Swiss-type."],[200.0,"And a large part, complex from every side: a mill- turn with a B axis, or 5-axis milling."],[208.0,"There are design rules here too."],[210.0,"Design holes and flats so the live tools can reach them."],[213.0,"Use standard sizes of holes, threads and slots."],[217.0,"On Swiss-type, keep the largest diameter of the part close to the bar diameter."],[222.0,"And cross holes that meet inside the part leave a burr, so plan how it will be removed."],[228.0,"These machines have their typical problems too."],[231.0,"mainly in soft materials, so good chip breaking is needed."],[238.0,"Chatter when milling with weak live tools."],[240.0,"On Swiss-type: a worn bushing, or an inaccurate bar, causing diameter errors."],[246.0,"And dimensional drift in long runs, because of heat."],[250.0,"What makes work on these machines expensive?"],[252.0,"The price of the machine and the hourly rate."],[255.0,"Programming and setup time, especially on Swiss- type, where there are many tools to set."],[261.0,"So in small quantities, the setup can cost more than the machining."],[265.0,"are no secondary operations, and in large quantities the cost per part is very low."],[273.0,"A mill-turn center produces a complex round part, completely finished, in one setup."],[279.0,"parts, with micron accuracy and in large quantities."],[287.0,"In the next chapter: honing and lapping."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch23_Honing_and_Lapping"]={"t":"Honing and lapping","l":[[1.0,"A hydraulic cylinder, an engine cylinder, a gauge block, and a valve sealing face."],[7.0,"All of these need a surface more precise and smoother than ordinary grinding delivers."],[13.0,"This is where two fine finishing processes come in: honing and lapping."],[18.0,"Honing is used mainly to finish bores."],[21.0,"The honing head carries several abrasive stones, which expand outward and press on the bore wall."],[27.0,"The head rotates and at the same time strokes up and down."],[31.0,"The combination creates a pattern of crossing lines on the wall, a crosshatch."],[36.0,"In an engine cylinder, these lines hold oil, and help the piston move freely."],[41.0,"Honing removes very little material, usually a few hundredths of a millimeter, with low heat."],[47.0,"Honing corrects the geometry of the bore: roundness, cylindricity and straightness."],[53.0,"It gives a very precise diameter, and a smooth surface, usually an Ra of 0.1 to 0.4 microns."],[61.0,"But, like a reamer, it follows the axis of the bore, and doesn't correct its position."],[67.0,"that flattens the peaks and leaves the valleys for oil."],[75.0,"during the work, and the process stops exactly at size."],[82.0,"surfaces that must be flat and parallel to the highest level."],[89.0,"Between the part and a lap plate, usually cast iron, there's a slurry of abrasive grains in a liquid."],[96.0,"The part moves over the plate in a constantly changing path, so every point wears evenly."],[102.0,"The grains roll and remove material at the level of microns."],[107.0,"The result: flatness measured in fractions of a micron, and a very smooth surface."],[113.0,"Flatness is checked with an optical flat, which shows bands of light on the surface of the part."],[118.0,"In double-sided lapping, both sides of the part are processed together, giving excellent parallelism."],[124.0,"It's important to tell lapping apart from polishing."],[128.0,"Polishing improves shine and smoothness, but doesn't correct the form."],[132.0,"Lapping improves the form itself: flatness, parallelism and size."],[138.0,"Honing: hydraulic cylinders, engine cylinders, gear bores, and injector parts."],[144.0,"Lapping: sealing faces of mechanical seals, valve seats, gauge blocks, and optical surfaces."],[152.0,"A small allowance is left, usually a few hundredths of a millimeter."],[156.0,"The previous process must be good: honing and lapping correct a little, and can't save a bad part."],[162.0,"the drawing, and for honing, the crosshatch angle if it matters."],[170.0,"And cleaning is very important: grains left on the part can ruin the assembly."],[175.0,"There are typical problems here too."],[177.0,"A bore that opens up at the ends, bell mouth, when the stroke overruns the bore too much."],[183.0,"A tapered bore, when the stroke isn't balanced."],[186.0,"Grains embedded in soft material."],[188.0,"In lapping: rounded edges, and scratches from an oversized grain that got into the slurry."],[195.0,"What makes honing and lapping expensive?"],[197.0,"The processes are slow, and remove very little each time."],[201.0,"Precise measurement, sometimes in a climate- controlled room."],[205.0,"Thorough cleaning of the part after the work."],[207.0,"On the other hand, lapping many parts together on one plate lowers the cost a lot."],[212.0,"And often, it's the only way to reach the required geometry and finish."],[218.0,"Honing finishes bores, giving them precise geometry and a pattern that holds oil."],[223.0,"Lapping finishes surfaces, giving flatness and parallelism at the level of fractions of a micron."],[230.0,"In the next chapter, the last in the series: additive manufacturing."]]};
window.MH_TR["Ch24_Additive_Manufacturing"]={"t":"Additive manufacturing","l":[[1.0,"So far we've seen processes that remove material, form it, or cast it."],[6.0,"In additive manufacturing, the part is built layer by layer, adding material only where it's needed."],[12.0,"structures, and single parts replacing whole assemblies."],[21.0,"methods, the processing after printing, and what makes it expensive."],[28.0,"We start from a 3D model of the part."],[30.0,"Software slices the model into thin layers, and calculates the shape of each layer."],[36.0,"The machine builds one layer, lowers or moves the part, and builds the next layer on top of it."],[42.0,"metals three main ones: powder bed fusion, directed energy deposition, and binder jetting."],[51.0,"This is the most common method for precise metal parts."],[54.0,"A recoater spreads a thin layer of metal powder, a few tens of microns thick."],[59.0,"A laser beam melts the powder only in the shape of the current layer."],[64.0,"The build platform lowers by one layer thickness, a new layer is spread, and the cycle repeats."],[70.0,"Everything happens in an inert gas atmosphere, usually argon, so the metal doesn't oxidize."],[77.0,"Surfaces that overhang at too steep an angle need supports."],[82.0,"The supports also conduct heat away, and hold the part so it doesn't warp."],[86.0,"The part is fused to the build plate, and at the end it's cut off, often by wire EDM."],[92.0,"There's also an electron beam version, ina vacuum, which suits titanium especially well."],[98.0,"is fed straight into a melt pool, created by a laser or an electric arc."],[108.0,"The head moves and builds the part, like welding, layer on layer."],[112.0,"The method is fast and suits large parts, but it's less accurate."],[116.0,"So the part is built close to its final shape, and then machined."],[120.0,"It's also used to repair expensive parts, like turbine blades and molds."],[125.0,"In binder jetting, a print head jets binder onto a layer of powder, without melting it."],[131.0,"Then the part is sintered in a furnace, and the particles fuse together."],[135.0,"During sintering the part shrinks, by about 20 percent, and the design must compensate for that."],[141.0,"The method is faster and cheaper, and suits batches of small parts."],[146.0,"There are several methods in plastics too."],[149.0,"FDM extrudes a molten plastic filament, and it's common in every shop."],[154.0,"SLA cures a liquid resin with light, and gives fine detail."],[159.0,"And SLS melts nylon powder, giving strong parts with no supports."],[164.0,"In the shop, the common uses are prototypes, and fixtures and gauges for production."],[170.0,"The part that comes out of the machine isn't finished yet."],[173.0,"First, a stress-relief heat treatment is done, even before it's cut off the plate."],[178.0,"Then the supports are removed, by hand or by machining."],[182.0,"For critical parts, HIP is used: high pressure and high temperature, which close internal porosity."],[189.0,"The surface is rough, usually an Ra of 5 to 15 microns, so functional surfaces are machined."],[196.0,"scan, which also sees porosity and internal channels."],[203.0,"channels, lattices, and surfaces that can't be machined."],[210.0,"wherever it doesn't carry load, and the part gets lighter."],[217.0,"Part consolidation: an assembly of ten parts can become a single part."],[222.0,"No tools and no molds, so you can change and try things quickly."],[226.0,"channels that follow the shape of the part greatly shorten the cycle time."],[233.0,"The process is slow, and part size is limited by the machine size."],[238.0,"The cost per part is high, and doesn't drop much with volume."],[242.0,"The properties can differ in different directions, depending on the layer direction."],[246.0,"There's a risk of porosity, internal stresses and distortion."],[251.0,"And in aerospace and medical, qualifying a printed part takes a lot of testing."],[256.0,"Additive manufacturing has its own design rules too."],[260.0,"Surfaces up to about 45 degrees from vertical are built without supports."],[265.0,"Avoid large flat surfaces hanging in the air."],[268.0,"Internal cavities need holes to remove the powder."],[271.0,"A uniform wall thickness is better, so heat spreads evenly."],[276.0,"The orientation of the part in the machine affects time, supports, surface finish and strength."],[282.0,"And on functional surfaces, a machining allowance is left."],[286.0,"What makes additive manufacturing expensive?"],[289.0,"Machine time, set mainly by the build height, meaning the number of layers."],[294.0,"The powder, and the inert gas."],[296.0,"Post-processing, which can reach half the cost."],[299.0,"And inspection, especially for critical parts."],[303.0,"On the other hand, several parts in the same build share the machine time."],[307.0,"complex parts, in small quantities, or when there's no other way to make the shape."],[315.0,"Additive manufacturing doesn't replace the processes we've learned; it joins them."],[321.0,"shape, and finish by 5-axis milling, turning, grinding or EDM."],[329.0,"Additive manufacturing builds a part layer by layer, giving design freedom no other process has."],[336.0,"It's slow and costly per part, but on the right part it changes the whole picture."],[341.0,"casting and forging, through machining and sheet metal, to the advanced processes."],[349.0,"For every process, we saw what it delivers, how to design for it, and what makes it expensive."],[355.0,"And the right choice is always a balance of shape, material, accuracy, quantity and cost."]]};
